Python has 35 reserved keywords in Python 3. These words have special meanings in the language and cannot be used as ordinary variable names.

Here is the complete list of Python keywords.

### 1\. All Python reserved keywords

| No. | Keyword  | Function                            |
| --- | -------- | ----------------------------------- |
| 1   | False    | Boolean false value                 |
| 2   | None     | Represents no value                 |
| 3   | True     | Boolean true value                  |
| 4   | and      | Logical AND                         |
| 5   | as       | Creates an alias                    |
| 6   | assert   | Tests a condition                   |
| 7   | async    | Defines asynchronous operations     |
| 8   | await    | Waits for an asynchronous operation |
| 9   | break    | Exits a loop                        |
| 10  | class    | Defines a class                     |
| 11  | continue | Skips to the next loop iteration    |
| 12  | def      | Defines a function                  |
| 13  | del      | Deletes a reference or item         |
| 14  | elif     | Adds another conditional branch     |
| 15  | else     | Defines an alternative branch       |
| 16  | except   | Handles exceptions                  |
| 17  | finally  | Executes cleanup code               |
| 18  | for      | Creates a loop                      |
| 19  | from     | Imports from a module               |
| 20  | global   | Declares a global variable          |
| 21  | if       | Tests a condition                   |
| 22  | import   | Imports a module                    |
| 23  | in       | Tests membership                    |
| 24  | is       | Tests object identity               |
| 25  | lambda   | Creates an anonymous function       |
| 26  | nonlocal | Refers to an enclosing variable     |
| 27  | not      | Logical NOT                         |
| 28  | or       | Logical OR                          |
| 29  | pass     | Does nothing                        |
| 30  | raise    | Raises an exception                 |
| 31  | return   | Returns a function value            |
| 32  | try      | Starts exception handling           |
| 33  | while    | Creates a conditional loop          |
| 34  | with     | Manages a context                   |
| 35  | yield    | Produces generator values           |

### 2\. Additional soft keywords

Python also has soft keywords. These are words that have special meaning only in particular language contexts.

| Soft keyword | Purpose                                 |
| ------------ | --------------------------------------- |
| match        | Starts pattern matching                 |
| case         | Defines a pattern-matching case         |
| _            | Wildcard in pattern matching            |
| type         | Introduces a type alias in Python 3.12+ |

Unlike reserved keywords, soft keywords can sometimes be used as variable names.

### 3\. How to obtain the complete list directly from Python

Run this program:

```python
import keyword

print("PYTHON RESERVED KEYWORDS")

for word in keyword.kwlist:
    print(word)

print("\nTOTAL:", len(keyword.kwlist))

print("\nSOFT KEYWORDS")

for word in keyword.softkwlist:
    print(word)
```

### 4\. How to test whether a variable name is reserved

```python
import keyword

variable = "class"

if keyword.iskeyword(variable):
    print("Invalid: Reserved keyword")
else:
    print("Valid variable name")
```

Output:

```text
Invalid: Reserved keyword
```

### 5\. Practical cybersecurity application

When developing Python security-testing scripts, you can use keyword checking to validate variable names or user-provided identifiers.

For example:

```python
import keyword

def validate_variable(name):

    if not name.isidentifier():
        return "Invalid identifier"

    if keyword.iskeyword(name):
        return "Reserved keyword"

    return "Valid variable name"

print(validate_variable("class"))
print(validate_variable("username"))
print(validate_variable("123user"))
```

Expected output:

```text
Reserved keyword
Valid variable name
Invalid identifier
```

